    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\n**How should environment promotion work?** should promote the same immutable artifact through environments rather than rebuilding different code at each stage.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the source of truth, boundary, success path, failure path, and ownership. Choose the smallest design that satisfies the requirement before introducing additional infrastructure.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core algorithm, state transition, lookup, or policy so it is easy to explain in a senior interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface CacheEntry<T> {\n  key: string;\n  value: T;\n  expiresAt: number;\n}\n\nfunction readCache<T>(\n  entry: CacheEntry<T> | undefined,\n  now: number,\n): T | undefined {\n  if (!entry || entry.expiresAt <= now) {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n\n  return entry.value;\n}\n\nconst value = readCache(\n  {\n    key: \"users\",\n    value: [\"u1\"],\n    expiresAt: 2000,\n  },\n  1000,\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform APIs when they improve readability without hiding an important architectural trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst cache = new Map([\n  [\"users\", [\"u1\"]],\n]);\n\nconst value =\n  cache.get(\"users\") ?? [];\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **How should environment promotion work?** to promote the same immutable artifact through environments.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant cost and how it changes with growth, retries, users, data, events, or deployments. Then describe what you would cache, batch, partition, isolate, or measure.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty state, partial failure, stale data, retries, cancellation, unauthorized access, slow network, rollout failure, and recovery where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, source of truth, failure/recovery path, observability signal, and key trade-off.",
